This MVP attempts to create a UI app interactively within Srcbook, but does it by running a cell, which declaratively describes a UI app at a high level of functionality. The SDK is inspired by interval.com's one, designed to build very opinionated internal tooling, inter-twined with node backend code.

The `@srcbook/api` is now a "proxy" websocket server, which broadcasts events back to the `session:<id>` channel. This is because otherwise registration between the node process running `@srcbook/ui-sdk` and the frontend would rely on the server anyways. Down the line, we can likely create better channel hygiene and not re-broadcast all events like I do now.

Here is the TS cell creating the app:
```typescript
import { Action, io, UIApp } from '@srcbook/ui-sdk';

const greetingAction = new Action('greeting', async () => {
  const name = await io.input.text("What's your name?");
  const age = await io.input.text("How old are you?");
  const greeting = `### Hello, ${name}!\n\nYou are \`${age}\` years old.`;
  await io.display.markdown(greeting);
  return greeting;
});

// UIApp knows how to connect to the proxy WS server
// through an env var passed to it
const app = new UIApp(greetingAction);
app.run();
```

## Gif of experience (no design effort made)
![CleanShot 2024-08-29 at 20 43 17](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/48ff3e66-39df-4966-a82d-f0d02691ef95)

## Events

Currently, the node process running in the cell sends a serialized component spec with an `ui:io:await_response` event. The client renders this, and once the user submits the interaction, the web client sends a `ui:io:response` event which leads to a promise getting resolved, which leads to the code continuing it's execution.


## Ideas that I will explore further
- Need to try more complex DSL things. In particular grouping of fields
- Need to introduce "start" "stop" mechanics for the app
- Need to support cancellation flows
- Rethink the events, which are very simple right now.

This enables .Net developers to construct a Typed Godot Array with a `ReadOnlySpan<T>,` a more flexible alternative compared to `IEnumerable<T>` or `T[]`.

For convenience:
```csharp
using GodotIntArray = Godot.Collections.Array<int>;
```

This PR enables:

```csharp
// Stack-Allocated Array that does not cause heap allocations.
Span<int> myStackAllocatedSpan = stackalloc int [Random.Shared.Next(1, 20)];

foreach(ref int i in myStackAllocatedSpan )
    i = Random.Shared.Next(0, 50);

GodotIntArray myGodotArrayFromStackAllocatedSpan = new(myStackAllocatedSpan);

// From unmanaged memory.
Span<int> unmanagedMemory;
unsafe { unmanagedMemory = new ((int*)Marshal.AllocHGlobal(20 * sizeof(int)), 20); }
unmanagedMemory[0] = 20;

GodotIntArray myGodotArrayFromUnmanagedMemorySpan = new(myStackAllocatedSpan);
```

Overall, this PR addresses the need for a dedicated data transaction `T[]` when creating a typed Godot Array in C# under certain circumstances.

